  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 219 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nThursday night through Friday...The upper trough is forecast to move \ninto the Rockies, as a strong upper level ridge axis extends from \nTexas into the Great Lakes. This ridge/trough pattern will bring \nincreasing PWATS over 1.0 inches and surface dewpoints in the 50s. \nGusty south winds 20 to 30 mph overnight will keep lows very mild in \nthe mid to upper 50s. Windy Friday area wide, at 20 to 35 mph with \nlocal gusts to 45 mph. A partly cloudy sky with highs Friday 77 to \n86. \n\nFriday night through Sunday night, increased chances for showers, \nwith a few thunderstorms Friday night as a cold front arrives in the \nnorthwest late Friday night. A lead shortwave trough will lift into \nDakotas Saturday, while the main upper trough hangs back west of the \nFour Corners with a closed low back across Arizona. The main upper \ntrough and closed low will advance slowly east across the Central \nRockies and Four Corners Saturday night. The cold front is forecast \nto stall out across northwest Kansas through east central Nebraska \nSaturday night. As the upper trough/closed low moves very slowly \neast through Sunday night, showers chances will persist. While still \nseveral days out, the NBM 48 hour probability of 0.50 inch or \ngreater is 50 to 60 percent for areas east of Grant through O'Neill \nending 12Z Monday. This lines up similar to the 12Z GEFS ensemble, \nwhich shows rainfall amounts from 0.5 inch to near 1.0 inch. The \nECMWF is further west, east of a line from Ogallala through \nAinsworth. Highs cooling into the upper 50s to low 60s Saturday, and \nthe mid 50s Sunday.\n\nMonday and Tuesday...A northern stream trough will move into and \nthrough the region. This will bring a lingering chance for showers\nMonday and much colder air. H85 temperatures could be as cold as\n0 to -5C each day, as shown by the GFS with MEX guidance highs only\nin the 40s. The current forecast has highs in the low to mid 50s\neach day.",
